{
  // The Fastify/Node server. NodeNext because Node's own resolver runs the
  // emitted CommonJS, and `lib` comes from the neutral base — this config used
  // to inherit `dom`, `dom.iterable`, `jsx: preserve` and the Next TypeScript
  // plugin from a root config that was really a Next project configuration.
  // Nothing here used any of it: removing all four left the program unchanged.
  //
  // `@luke/core` has no `paths` alias and no project reference here. It
  // resolves by package name through the package's own `exports` map to
  // `dist/**/*.d.ts` — the same way `apps/web` and `packages/calendar` resolve
  // it, so one contract is exercised by every consumer.
  //
  // Both mechanisms had to go together, and neither was what it looked like.
  // The alias claimed to typecheck core from source, but a `references` entry
  // to a composite project outranks `paths`: TypeScript redirected the
  // resolution to `packages/core/dist`, so this program never saw core's
  // sources. What the alias did still do was answer `@luke/core/<anything>`,
  // including the subpaths the `exports` map does not publish — those
  // typechecked green against `dist` and fail at runtime with
  // ERR_PACKAGE_PATH_NOT_EXPORTED. Removing `references` alone would have
  // handed the alias its original meaning back and moved this program onto
  // core's sources; removing the alias alone would have left a second
  // resolution mechanism doing what package exports already do.
  //
  // Two consequences of having no reference, both accepted deliberately.
  //
  // On the command line, a missing or stale `packages/core/dist` surfaces as
  // TS2307 rather than the more explicit TS6305. Turbo's
  // `typecheck.dependsOn: ["^build"]` is what stops that from happening, in CI
  // and in `.husky/pre-push` alike.
  //
  // In the editor, tsserver no longer redirects core's declarations back to its
  // sources — a project reference is what enabled that, so with none, every
  // consumer here (and in `apps/web` and `packages/calendar`, which never had
  // one) reads `dist`. Rename a symbol in `packages/core/src` and this project
  // stays green in the IDE against the previous build until `dist` refreshes;
  // `pnpm dev` runs core's `tsc --watch`, which is what keeps that window
  // short. Reading the built declarations is the point — it is the contract
  // every runtime consumer resolves — so the reference is not coming back to
  // improve navigation.
  "extends": "../../tsconfig.base.json",
  "compilerOptions": {
    "composite": true,
    "module": "NodeNext",
    "moduleResolution": "NodeNext",
    "outDir": "./dist",
    "rootDir": "./src",
    "declaration": true,
    "declarationMap": true,
    "sourceMap": true,
    "types": ["node"],
    "paths": {
      "pdfmake/interfaces": ["./node_modules/@types/pdfmake/interfaces.d.ts"]
    }
  },
  "include": ["src/**/*"],
  "exclude": ["node_modules", "dist"]
}
